# Summary
Introduced: a reusable Raft peer dispatcher that treats non-secret frame identity plus CRC as sufficient validation before invoking privileged Raft RPC handlers. No security fix was found in this commit.
The commit adds public server-side dispatchers that decode a peer frame and immediately call OpenRaft operations such as append_entries, vote, pre_vote, transfer_leader, and install_full_snapshot. The only peer validation performed by the frame codec is equality of the frame's cluster name, source node id, and target node id against an expected identity. Those fields are serialized inside the frame itself and are not protected by a secret, signature, MAC, mTLS identity, or other authentication mechanism; the checksum is a CRC64 and only protects against accidental corruption. If this dispatcher is wired to a peer listener reachable by an untrusted host or process, an attacker who can guess or learn the cluster/node identifiers can forge valid frames and drive consensus state changes, force votes/leadership changes, append malicious log entries if Raft consistency checks permit, or install snapshots, causing control-plane integrity loss and denial of service. The commit does not appear to add the production listener in this patch, so immediate exposure depends on how this newly public dispatcher is used, but the introduced dispatcher is unsafe as an authentication boundary.

# Resolution
Fixed by the Raft peer-auth series, closed by commit [fa8a5fa4cddbf39bdb9564dc4f2ae7d0cd4df56e](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/fa8a5fa4cddbf39bdb9564dc4f2ae7d0cd4df56e) (`Require auth for multi-node Raft peer mode`).

The reachable experimental multi-node Unix-peer control-plane path no longer treats CRC-protected frame identity as an authentication boundary. Multi-node peer startup now requires `ARGMIN_CONTROL_PLANE_RAFT_AUTH_CREDENTIALS` covering the configured peer map. The peer worker decodes the auth envelope, verifies the scoped `RaftPeer` credential against cluster/source/target/operation, validates the authenticated payload matches the embedded peer frame identity and operation, and only then calls the OpenRaft append/vote/pre-vote/snapshot/transfer-leader dispatchers. Responses are also signed with reverse peer identity, and missing, malformed, wrong-cluster/source/target/role, bad-MAC, payload-bitflip, stale, and unknown-credential frames fail before OpenRaft dispatch and before checkpoint mutation.

The original raw identity+CRC dispatch helpers remain lower-level internal/test helpers, not the process-level trust boundary for multi-node peer mode. Single-node local peer-socket mode and low-level tests can still run without auth, but a configured multi-node process peer map fails closed without scoped credentials.

